            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "rough or smooth), colour and other noticeable features, and record your observations in the notebook. Group the collected pieces based on their appearance."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.143,
            0.744,
            0.242
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Materials that typically have shiny surfaces are said to have a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "lustrous",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " appearance. Such materials with lustre are usually metals. Examples of metals include iron, copper, zinc, aluminium, gold, etc. However, some metals may lose their lustre and start to look dull or non-lustrous due"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.243,
            0.593,
            0.362
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "to the effect of air and moisture on them. As a result, we often notice the lustre only on their freshly cut surfaces. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Non-lustrous",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " materials are those that do not have a shiny surface. Some examples of non-lustrous materials are paper, wood, rubber, jute, etc."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.61,
            0.283,
            0.775,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Are all lustrous materials metals?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.362,
            0.699,
            0.486
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "“All that glitters is not gold” goes an old saying! Not all the materials that shine are metals. Surfaces of some materials are made shiny by polishing or coating them with thin layers of plastic, wax or any other material which makes them look shiny. These materials may not be metals."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.694,
            0.311,
            0.899,
            0.459
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.694,
                0.311,
                0.899,
                0.459
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.502,
            0.493,
            0.525
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6.3.2 Which materials are hard?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.536,
            0.745,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When you press different objects or materials with your hands, some of them like stones, may be hard to compress, while others, like an eraser, can be easily compressed. Take a metal key and use it to scratch the surface of a piece of wood, aluminium, stone, iron, candle, chalk and any other material or object. Can some materials be scratched more easily than others? Materials which can be compressed or scratched easily are "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "soft",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", while other materials which are difficult to compress or scratch are "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "hard",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". However, these properties are relative in nature. For example, rubber is harder than sponge but softer than iron."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.768,
            0.411,
            0.79
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 6.5: Let us observe"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.801,
            0.744,
            0.883
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hold the objects given in Table 6.3 with your hands. Feel whether the objects are hard or soft. Find out the materials they are made up of. Enter your observations in Table 6.3."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "aside_text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.891,
            0.762,
            0.91,
            0.866
          ],
          "content": [
            {
